cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "OligoTox: evidence-gated toxicity and potency models for oligonucleotides"
message: "If you use this software or its findings, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Armanios
    given-names: Beshoy
    orcid: ""
repository-code: "https://github.com/barmanios123/OligoTox"
url: "https://oligotox.streamlit.app"
license: MIT
version: 0.2.0
date-released: "2026-10-27"
keywords:
  - antisense oligonucleotide
  - siRNA
  - preclinical toxicology
  - hepatotoxicity
  - neurotoxicity
  - model evaluation
  - data leakage
  - calibration
  - negative results
abstract: >-
  Sequence-based models for 13 preclinical oligonucleotide toxicity endpoints
  and for siRNA and ASO potency, each gated by the evidence that supports it.
  Of the 13 toxicity endpoints, 3 are presented as predictions and only CNS
  neurotoxicity has demonstrated external validity, having been validated by
  training on one assay and testing on another with no shared sequences.

  The project's main contribution is methodological rather than predictive.
  An earlier version scored PR-AUC 0.93 on hepatocellular injury; after
  removing curated annotations that leaked the outcome and grouping
  cross-validation folds by sequence, the honest figure is 0.29. Baselines
  then showed that the apparently strongest endpoint was recognising which
  dataset a row came from rather than the molecule, and an independent screen
  showed the headline endpoint does not transfer.

  The repository ships the audit harness that found these failures as a
  standalone tool applicable to any oligonucleotide dataset, and records nine
  reproducible failure modes in docs/NEGATIVE_RESULTS.md. Every published
  number is reproducible from the committed data.
